TI  - "I feel like you should buy an umbrella before it starts raining": Caregiver 
      Perspectives on Illness Inflection Points in COPD.
LID - aaoag094 [pii]
LID - 10.1093/annalsats/aaoag094 [doi]
AB  - RATIONALE: The prolonged and unpredictable trajectory of chronic obstructive 
      pulmonary disease (COPD) complicates prognostication and leaves caregivers 
      uncertain about what to expect. Although caregiver unmet needs are well 
      described, little is known about how they interpret missed or unclear prognostic 
      conversations or how informational and support needs evolve during concrete 
      illness transitions. OBJECTIVES: To examine how bereaved caregivers of people 
      with COPD perceive illness inflection points and how communication gaps and 
      multimorbidity shape their expectations and support needs. METHODS: We conducted 
      semi-structured interviews with bereaved caregivers of patients with any stage of 
      COPD who had been seen in Emory Healthcare pulmonary clinics and died of 
      respiratory causes within the prior 12 months. Two investigators independently 
      analyzed transcripts using template analysis. RESULTS: We interviewed 18 bereaved 
      caregivers (median age 58 years, 50% Black, 89% female). We identified two key 
      themes: (1) Navigating without a roadmap: caregivers want earlier, clearer 
      communication about what to expect as COPD progresses, and (2) Missing the 
      signposts: inflection points in the COPD illness trajectory are missed 
      opportunities for additional support. The initiation of supplemental oxygen was 
      described as a particularly visible and life-altering marker of advanced disease, 
      triggering loss of independence and reshaping daily life for patients and 
      caregivers. CONCLUSION: Bereaved caregivers emphasized the need for earlier, more 
      specific prognostic conversations that focus on functional decline and care needs 
      rather than time remaining. Clearer expectations about the illness course and 
      help distinguishing COPD progression from other comorbidities may enable 
      caregivers to recognize illness transitions as meaningful signals. Recognizing 
      oxygen initiation and other illness inflection points as actionable indicators 
      could help clinicians provide timely prognostic information, anticipatory 
      guidance, and supportive care to patients and families across the COPD 
      trajectory.
