TI  - Clinicians' Perspectives on Family Health Literacy: A Qualitative Study of Family 
      Engagement in the ICU.
LID - aaoag210 [pii]
LID - 10.1093/annalsats/aaoag210 [doi]
AB  - RATIONALE: Engaging family members in care is part of a patient- and 
      family-centered approach in intensive care units (ICUs), but little is known 
      about how ICU clinicians perceive family health literacy and its impact on family 
      engagement. OBJECTIVE(S): The purpose of this study was to explore clinician 
      perspectives on family health literacy in the ICU. METHODS: We conducted a 
      secondary analysis of qualitative data, collected on interprofessional teamwork 
      in medical and surgical ICUs across three hospitals in the U.S. Data included 
      57.6 hours of observations, 17 shadowing experiences, and 41 semi-structured 
      interviews with clinicians and family members. We identified health literacy 
      among family members as an emergent theme and therefore chose to conduct a 
      thematic analysis to characterize perceptions of family literacy in the ICU. We 
      iteratively coded the data until no new themes emerged and consensus was reached 
      among the research team. RESULTS: Three main themes were identified: (1) 
      Clinicians assess family members' health literacy and judge their ability to 
      engage in care, (2) Family members' participation in the care process is affected 
      by clinicians' assessments and perceptions of family health literacy, and (3) 
      Clinicians employ strategies to bridge perceived gaps in health literacy. 
      Clinicians evaluated family members' health literacy, explicitly or implicitly, 
      how family members access, understand, appraise, and apply health information. 
      Their perceptions affected the degree to which they sought or permitted family 
      involvement in care. When clinicians perceived family members as having lower 
      health literacy, they were skeptical of family members' participation. However, 
      clinicians also saw family members as offering valuable information that can 
      guide care and decision-making. Clinicians described strategies to engage family 
      members in the decision-making process by inviting them to medical rounds, 
      providing updates through consultation, and phone calls. CONCLUSIONS: The study 
      findings reflected clinicians' perceptions towards family health literacy and 
      highlighted the importance of their roles in engaging family members. Further 
      studies should explore the factors influencing clinicians' assessments of family 
      health literacy and develop strategies to enhance equitable family engagement in 
      the ICU.
