TI  - The child opportunity index and discharge disposition among children with new 
      tracheostomy and long-term mechanical ventilation.
PG  - 773-781
LID - 10.1093/annalsats/aaoag025 [doi]
AB  - RATIONALE: Although discharge to home is the goal for many children on new 
      long-term mechanical ventilation via tracheostomy (LTMV-T), families often face 
      barriers to access, such as home health nursing. Disparities in discharge 
      disposition and the impact of social determinants of health (SDOHs) remain 
      understudied. OBJECTIVE: To examine the association between neighborhood-level 
      SDOHs and discharge disposition among children on new LMTV-T. METHODS: This 
      retrospective cohort study included children aged 0 to 18 years newly discharged 
      with LTMV-T between 2014 and 2023 from 47 pediatric hospitals, using the 
      Pediatric Health Information System database. The primary outcome was discharge 
      disposition, defined as home or skilled facility. The primary exposure was the 
      Child Opportunity Index (COI), a composite measure of neighborhood-level SDOHs, 
      reported in quintiles (very low to very high opportunity). The association 
      between COI and discharge disposition was evaluated using a multivariable 
      mixed-effects logistic regression model with a random effect for hospitals and 
      states. A mediation analysis was conducted to assess whether COI mediated racial 
      and ethnic disparities in discharge disposition. RESULTS: Of the 5042 eligible 
      children, most were male (57%), covered by government insurance (61%), and 
      non-Hispanic White (45%) and non-Hispanic Black (23%). Median length of stay was 
      148 days (IQR, 81-232 days), and median age at discharge was 10 months (IQR, 7-18 
      months). A total of 1011 (20%) children were discharged to a skilled facility, 
      and 2554 (51%) lived in very low to low COI neighborhoods, with more than half of 
      the non-Hispanic Black population living in very low to low COI neighborhoods. 
      Compared to children from very high opportunity neighborhoods, children from very 
      low COI (adjusted odds ratio [aOR] = 3.11; 95% CI, 2.30-4.21), low COI 
      (aOR = 2.22; 95% CI, 1.63-3.02), moderate COI (aOR = 2.19; 95% CI, 1.60-2.99), 
      and high COI (aOR = 1.65; 95% CI, 1.20-2.26) had increased odds of being 
      discharged to skilled facility than home. Among non-Hispanic Black children, only 
      24% of the total effect between race/ethnicity and discharge disposition was 
      mediated by COI. CONCLUSION: Disparities in institutionalization exist among 
      children with LTMV-T from lower COI compared to very high COI neighborhoods. 
      Addressing neighborhood-level conditions and implementing policies such as paid 
      family caregiving or insurance reimbursement may reduce inequities in discharge 
      disposition.
