PMID- 42127990
OWN - NLM
STAT- Publisher
LR  - 20260513
IS  - 2325-6621 (Electronic)
IS  - 2325-6621 (Linking)
DP  - 2026 May 13
TI  - Poor Sleep Quality and Heightened Perceptual Responses to Exercise in Community 
      Dwellers: Results from the CanCOLD Study.
LID - aaoag120 [pii]
LID - 10.1093/annalsats/aaoag120 [doi]
AB  - RATIONALE: Poor sleep quality is associated with poor health outcomes, while 
      exercise is protective of health. However, understanding of the impact of poor 
      sleep on exercise tolerance and capacity in community-dwelling adults is limited. 
      OBJECTIVES: To explore associations between sleep quality and exercise in a 
      community-dwelling adult population (age > 40 y). METHODS: We analyzed a 
      cross-sectional sample of adults from the CanCOLD study, a prospective cohort 
      study conducted in adults > 40y containing demographic and cardiopulmonary 
      exercise (CPET) outcomes. We used adjusted multiple linear regression to 
      determine associations between Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI) and CPET 
      variables at peak exercise. Logistic regression determined the odds of poor 
      sleepers (PSQI > 5) compared to good sleepers (PSQI ≤ 5) having abnormally high 
      dyspnea (>ULN) or abnormal physiologic CPET outcomes (<LLN or > ULN). 
      MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS: In 1072 analyzed individuals (425 poor sleepers; 
      age 66.4 ± 9.7 years; 40.5% female), poor sleepers were more likely to experience 
      abnormally high levels of dyspnea at peak CPET (dyspnea > ULN for work rate odds 
      ratio [95% CI] 1.65 [1.21-2.24], for ⩒E 1.52 [1.07-2.17]), but no differences in 
      the odds of having abnormal key physiologic variables at peak CPET (⩒O2peak, HR, 
      O2 pulse normal). Dyspnea adjusted for ⩒E or ⩒O2 positively correlated, and 
      percent-predicted work rate negatively correlated, with PSQI (ie, worse sleep; 
      both P < .05), while most physiologic variables showed no correlation. 
      CONCLUSIONS: Perceptual responses to exercise were impacted more by poor sleep 
      quality than physiological outcomes. These data highlight the importance of 
      adequate sleep quality to lessen the burden of uncomfortable activity-related 
      sensations.
CI  - © The Author(s) 2026. Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of the 
      American Thoracic Society.
