VI  - 18
IP  - 5
DP  - 2021 May
TI  - Ultrasound for Pleural Disease. Beyond a Pocket of Pleural Fluid.
PG  - 749-756
LID - 10.1513/AnnalsATS.202008-948CME [doi]
AB  - The evolution of pleural disease imaging modalities through the years has helped 
      the scientific community understand and treat various disease states. Ultrasound 
      (US) has been an image modality that has reigned superior to those used in the 
      past such as chest X-ray and computed tomographic scan in terms of cost 
      effectiveness, portability, and reduction in unwarranted radiation exposure to 
      patients. Here we provide a succinct review of US use in pleural disease 
      including imaging techniques, identifying safe pleural space for access, and 
      predicting pleural fluid volume and etiology along with specificities regarding 
      trapped lung identification and pleural mass biopsy. We believe bedside chest US 
      is an adjunct to the physical exam adding superior diagnostic abilities. Further 
      research is warranted in more specific aspects of sonographic use such as in 
      fibrinolytic therapy management, evaluation for trapped lung, and the utility of 
      specific modes like the color flow Doppler.
