VI  - 14
IP  - 9
DP  - 2017 Sep
TI  - Supplemental Oxygen in Interstitial Lung Disease: An Art in Need of Science.
PG  - 1373-1377
LID - 10.1513/AnnalsATS.201702-137OI [doi]
AB  - Interstitial lung disease (ILD) comprises a large and heterogeneous group of 
      disorders that often lead to progressive fibrosis and premature death. Oxygen 
      supplementation is typically used in patients with advanced lung disease with 
      resting hypoxemia; however, there is a paucity of evidence guiding the use of 
      supplemental oxygen in ILD, and significant heterogeneity in clinical practice. 
      It remains unclear whether supplemental oxygen improves clinically meaningful 
      outcomes, and the role of ambulatory oxygen supplementation in isolated 
      exertional hypoxemia is particularly controversial. In some regions, the lack of 
      robust data creates barriers to funding support and access to supplemental oxygen 
      for patients with ILD. Further research into the role of oxygen supplementation 
      is needed to optimize the comprehensive care of this patient population.
