TI  - Control of regulatory T cells and airway tolerance by lung macrophages and 
      dendritic cells.
PG  - S306-13
LID - 10.1513/AnnalsATS.201401-028AW [doi]
AB  - Airway tolerance, a state of immunological surveillance, suppresses the 
      development of lung inflammatory disorders that are driven by various 
      pathological effector cells of the immune system. Tolerance in the lung to 
      inhaled antigens is primarily mediated by regulatory T cells (Treg cells) that 
      can inhibit effector T cells via a myriad of mechanisms. Accumulating evidence 
      suggests that regulatory antigen-presenting cells are critical for generating 
      Treg cells and/or maintaining the suppressive environment in the lung. This 
      review focuses on the control of airway tolerance by Treg cells and the role of 
      regulatory lung tissue and alveolar macrophages, and lung and lymph node 
      dendritic cells, in contributing to airway tolerance that is associated with 
      suppression of allergic asthmatic disease.
