VI  - 15
IP  - Suppl 3
DP  - 2018 Nov
TI  - Mucociliary Defense: Emerging Cellular, Molecular, and Animal Models.
PG  - S210-S215
LID - 10.1513/AnnalsATS.201806-439AW [doi]
AB  - Respiratory tissues are bombarded by billions of particles daily. If allowed to 
      accumulate, these particles can cause injury, inflammation, or infection, and 
      thus may significantly disrupt airflow and gas exchange. Mucociliary defense, a 
      primary mechanism for protecting host tissues, operates through the coordinated 
      functions of mucus and cilia that trap and eliminate inhaled materials. 
      Mucociliary function is also required for the elimination of endogenous cells and 
      debris. Although defense is necessarily robust, it is also tightly regulated to 
      minimize physiologic disruption of the host. Indeed, mucociliary dysfunction 
      contributes to the pathogenesis of many lung diseases-including asthma, chronic 
      obstructive pulmonary disease, pulmonary fibrosis, and cystic fibrosis-in which 
      airflow limitation, inflammation, persistent tissue injury, and structural 
      remodeling occur. Here, we highlight recent advances in cilia and mucin biology, 
      the importance of well-controlled mucociliary interactions, and the need to 
      better understand how these regulate innate barrier and immune defense.
