TI  - Cardiopulmonary Exercise Test in Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy.
PG  - S102-S109
LID - 10.1513/AnnalsATS.201611-884FR [doi]
AB  - Understanding the functional limitation in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, the most 
      common inherited heart disease, is challenging. In addition to the occurrence of 
      disease-related complications, several factors are potential determinants of 
      exercise limitation, including left ventricular hypertrophy, myocardial fiber 
      disarray, left ventricular outflow tract obstruction, microvascular ischemia, and 
      interstitial fibrosis. Furthermore, drugs commonly used in the daily management 
      of these patients may interfere with exercise capacity, especially those with a 
      negative chronotropic effect. Cardiopulmonary exercise testing can safely and 
      objectively evaluate the functional capacity of these patients and help the 
      physician in understanding the mechanisms that underlie this limitation. Features 
      that reduce exercise capacity may predict progression to heart failure in these 
      patients and even the risk of sudden cardiac death.
