TI  - The regulation of pulmonary inflammation by the hypoxia-inducible 
      factor-hydroxylase oxygen-sensing pathway.
PG  - S271-6
LID - 10.1513/AnnalsATS.201403-108AW [doi]
AB  - Although the hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF)-hydroxylase oxygen-sensing pathway 
      has been extensively reviewed in the context of cellular responses to hypoxia and 
      cancer biology, its importance in regulating innate immune biology is less well 
      described. In this review, we focus on the role of the HIF-hydroxylase pathway in 
      regulating myeloid cell responses and its relevance to inflammatory lung disease. 
      The more specific roles of individual HIF/ prolyl hydroxylase pathway members in 
      vivo are discussed in the context of lineage-specific rodent models of 
      inflammation, with final reference made to the therapeutic challenges of 
      targeting the HIF/hydroxylase pathway in immune cells.
